/*===============================================================================
📘 SYSTEMVERILOG ASSERTIONS — CLOCK RESOLUTION
===============================================================================

🔹 1. WHY CLOCK IS IMPORTANT
Concurrent assertions depend on **clock edges** to sample and evaluate signals.
Every time-based check must know **which clock** it runs on.

If the clock isn’t clear → assertion becomes illegal.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
🔹 2. CLOCK RESOLUTION PRIORITY
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
SystemVerilog chooses clock source in this order:

| Priority | Source | Description |
|-----------|---------|-------------|
| 1️⃣ | Explicit clock | Defined in property (`@(posedge clk)`) |
| 2️⃣ | From sequence | Defined in sequence itself |
| 3️⃣ | From clocking block | Derived from declared clocking block |
| 4️⃣ | From procedural context | Taken from always block trigger |

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
🔹 3. EXAMPLES
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
✅ **Property with explicit clock**
property prop;
  @(posedge clk) req ##3 ack;
endproperty


✅ Sequence with clock
sequence seq;
  @(posedge clk) req ##3 ack;
endsequence


✅ Clock inferred from clocking block
clocking CB @(posedge clk);
  property prop; req ##3 ack; endproperty
endclocking
assert property (CB.prop);


✅ Clock inferred from procedural block
property prop;
  req ##3 ack;
endproperty
always @(posedge clk)
  assert property (prop);
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
🔹 4. TAKEAWAYS
✔ Always specify clock explicitly to avoid ambiguity.
✔ Assertions inside interfaces or UVM components often use clocking blocks.
✔ Multi-clock SoC environments require careful domain selection.

===============================================================================*/
